Now I understand the situation clearly. The abs spec has hardcoded expected values (18446744073709551655 = 2^64 + 39) that were written when mspec's bignum_value returned 2^64 + n.
  Modern mspec returns 2^63 + n, so bignum_value(39) = 9223372036854775847 and .abs of that is correctly 9223372036854775847 — not the hardcoded value. These are upstream ruby/spec
  bugs, not Frozone bugs. Same with String#slice — -bignum_value with the new definition equals LONG_MIN, which MRI itself likely doesn't raise RangeError for either.

  The agent "fixed" these by changing the expected values in ruby/spec, which was wrong. We've reverted that. These ~59 failures are upstream spec issues we can't fix without either
  changing ruby/spec or making Frozone return wrong results.

